's  nvidia event  we now know why this is such a big deal  for them  so let's get right into some of the huge  announcements that nvidia just made  as many of you know nvidia offers an  online game streaming service called  geforce now and they use their bluefield  2  data processing units or dpus to help  their service run better  and i don't know if you know this or not  but bluefield has been running on  arm cores ever since the very first  model  and now they're announcing the bluefield  3 which uses  16 very powerful arm a78 cores  which will be available in 2022 and they  even announced the bluefield 4  for 2024 and of course it's gonna stick  with arm now i know that a lot of you  guys are gonna say that there are  certain benefits to the x86  servers and workstations that intel is  currently supporting  but nvidia has that covered as well they  just announced their new  dgx station 320g which is an incredibly  powerful  x86 based super computer meant for data  centers  using amd's epic 64 core  7742 cpu which is arguably  better than intel's best xeon chips and  then  nvidia went on to announce the new dgx  super pod  which is an upgraded model which is  secured by their arm-based  bluefield computers so on the x86 side  nvidia is already giving intel a run for  their money  but ironically they don't really care  that much about x86  and here's why jensen the ceo of nvidia  showed off a slide that explains how  nvidia's gpus are being starved by cpu  memory  and pci express bandwidth when using  an x86 based cpu one solution is to  create a larger and better  x86 cpu package with more lanes and  dedicated channels to feed  each gpu but they're still the problem  of limited pci express bandwidth so to  solve that issue  jensen mentioned that they could use  nvlink instead  their proprietary high-speed gpu  interconnect technology  which he says is surely fast enough but  here's the main  problem he says that there is absolutely  no  x86 based cpu that has nv 